        <h2 id="replacement">1. With vs. Without Replacement (The One Setting That Matters Most)</h2>
        <p>
          Almost every awkward pause during a live number draw traces back to one toggle: whether a called number stays on the wheel or leaves it. Before you press Spin on Ball #1 or Ticket #1, decide which statistical rule fits your room:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>With Replacement (<em>Remove Winner After Spin = OFF</em>):</strong> Every spin is an independent event with exact probability <code>1 / N</code>. If the number <code>7</code> lands twice in a row on a 1–10 wheel, its chance on spin three is still exactly 10%. Keep this OFF for <a href="/random-number-1-20">D20 tabletop rolls</a>, classroom multiplication warm-ups, repeated <a href="/1-5-random-number-generator">1–5 station checks</a>, and independent probability trials.</li>
          <li><strong>Without Replacement (<em>Remove Winner After Spin = ON</em>):</strong> Once a slice wins, it immediately leaves the wheel so the remaining <code>N − 1</code> slices divide 100% of the canvas evenly. Turn this ON before calling <a href="/bingo-number-generator-1-75">75-ball Bingo</a>, revealing <a href="/fantasy-draft-order-wheel">fantasy draft slots</a>, seeding a <a href="/number-generator-1-to-16">16-team bracket</a>, or drawing multi-prize <a href="/raffle-wheel">ticket raffles</a>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Wait—what if you forget to toggle removal ON before the very first spin of a charity raffle? Don't restart the whole draw. Click the <strong>Remove Winner &amp; Continue</strong> button right inside the winner popup, then switch <em>Remove Winner After Spin</em> ON in Settings so every spin from #2 onward clears automatically.
        </p>

