        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Load your format preset:</strong> Keep <em>Classic 75-Ball (B1–O75)</em> for North American cards, tap <em>UK 90-Ball (1–90)</em> for British ticket strips, or pick <em>Speed Bingo (1–30)</em> for short rounds.</li>
          <li><strong>Switch ON Remove Winner After Spin:</strong> Check this before calling Ball #1 so no ball can ever repeat inside the same game.</li>
          <li><strong>Drop Spin Duration to 2 seconds:</strong> A 6-second suspense spin feels great for a grand prize raffle, but across 45 Bingo calls it adds three full minutes of dead air. Set Spin Duration to <code>2s</code>, uncheck <em>Show Winner Popup Modal</em> if you want continuous rapid-fire spins, and press <strong>Spacebar</strong> once players finish marking their cards.</li>
          <li><strong>Verify every "BINGO!" from Spin History:</strong> When a player shouts Bingo, ask them to read back the five winning squares while you check the scrollable <strong>Spin History</strong> panel (or click <em>Export CSV</em> for a clean audit list). If they missed a call, keep spinning without resetting!</li>
        </ol>

        <h2 id="pools-tickets">4. 100-Square Sports Pools &amp; Paper Raffle Ticket Stubs</h2>
        <p>
          Two real-world setups cause more headaches than almost any other number draw: assigning random digits to a <strong>10×10 sports pool grid</strong> (Super Bowl, March Madness, World Cup) and drawing <strong>pre-printed paper raffle rolls</strong> that don't start at <code>1</code>.
        </p>
        <h3>Setting Up a 100-Square Pool Fairly in Two Minutes</h3>
        <p>
          Never fill the 0–9 row and column headers in order or let one person pick digits off the top of their head. Once all 100 boxes are claimed by participants:
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Open <a href="/number-picker-wheel">Number Picker Wheel</a>, click <strong>Clear All</strong>, and bulk-paste digits <code>0</code> through <code>9</code> (ten lines).</li>
          <li>Switch ON <em>Remove Winner After Spin</em> and screen-share the canvas (or record one uncut phone video for the group chat).</li>
          <li>Spin ten times to assign the horizontal team's columns from left to right (Column 1 = Spin 1, Column 2 = Spin 2, through Column 10).</li>
          <li>Click <strong>Reset to Default</strong> (or re-paste <code>0–9</code>) and spin ten more times for the vertical team's rows from top to bottom. Click <strong>Export CSV</strong> so every player has timestamped proof of both axes.</li>
        </ol>

        <h3>Drawing Pre-Printed Ticket Rolls (e.g., #401 to #750)</h3>
        <p>
          Physical carnival and PTA ticket rolls rarely start at 0001—someone hands you a leftover roll starting at <code>401</code> and ending at <code>750</code>. Instead of subtracting 400 in your head on stage:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>In Excel or Google Sheets, type <code>401</code> in cell A1 and <code>402</code> in cell A2, then drag the corner handle down to <code>750</code> (350 numbers total—well inside our 500-entry browser limit).</li>
          <li>Copy column A, open <a href="/number-picker-wheel">Number Picker Wheel</a>, click <strong>Bulk Paste Entries</strong>, paste, and hit Save Custom Wheel.</li>
          <li>If your event uses a <strong>"must be present to win"</strong> rule and ticket #582 goes unclaimed after 60 seconds, keep elimination mode ON and spin again—both the unclaimed stub and the verified winner stay documented in order inside your CSV export.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="draft-raffles">5. Fantasy Draft Lotteries, Worked Weight Math &amp; Gift Swaps</h2>
        <p>
          Numeric order also powers some of the most watched moments in friend groups and offices each year:
        </p>
        <h3>Worked Example: A 6-Team Weighted Keeper League Draft Lottery</h3>
        <p>
          In dynasty and keeper leagues, non-playoff teams get better odds at the #1 overall pick to discourage tanking while still helping rebuilding rosters. On the <a href="/fantasy-draft-order-wheel">Fantasy Draft Order Wheel</a>, enter the six non-playoff managers once, then tap the <code>+</code> weight button beside each name instead of pasting duplicates:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>12th Place Manager:</strong> Weight <code>6x</code> &rarr; <code>6 / 17 = 35.29%</code> chance at the top pick</li>
          <li><strong>11th Place Manager:</strong> Weight <code>4x</code> &rarr; <code>4 / 17 = 23.53%</code></li>
          <li><strong>10th Place Manager:</strong> Weight <code>3x</code> &rarr; <code>3 / 17 = 17.65%</code></li>
          <li><strong>9th Place Manager:</strong> Weight <code>2x</code> &rarr; <code>2 / 17 = 11.76%</code></li>
          <li><strong>8th Place Manager:</strong> Weight <code>1x</code> &rarr; <code>1 / 17 = 5.88%</code></li>
          <li><strong>7th Place Manager:</strong> Weight <code>1x</code> &rarr; <code>1 / 17 = 5.88%</code></li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Every slice expands proportionally on screen and displays its exact live percentage right beside the name. For standard redraft leagues with equal <code>1x</code> odds across 10 or 12 managers, turn ON elimination mode and use the <strong>Reverse Countdown</strong> format: Spin #1 reveals Pick #12, Spin #2 reveals Pick #11, counting all the way up until the final two managers stare at a 50/50 split for Pick #1.
        </p>
        <p>
          The exact same weighted <code>1x–20x</code> setup shines on <a href="/raffle-wheel">Raffle Wheel</a> (when raffle bundles give buyers 3 or 5 tickets) and <a href="/prize-wheel">Prize Wheel</a> (when a booth wants 10% coupons weighted at <code>6x</code> and a scarce grand prize at <code>1x</code>). For holiday parties, draw turn order 1–16 on <a href="/number-generator-1-to-16">Number Generator 1 to 16</a> before switching to steal/swap action twists on <a href="/gift-exchange-wheel">Gift Exchange Wheel</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="classroom-labs">6. A 25-Minute Classroom Probability Lab Using CSV Export</h2>
        <p>
          Textbooks tell students that a fair 10-sided spinner yields each digit 10% of the time—yet students rarely feel the difference between <strong>short-run streaks</strong> and the <strong>Law of Large Numbers</strong> until they collect real numbers themselves. Here is a 25-minute lesson script using the <a href="/pick-a-number-between-1-and-10">Pick a Number 1 to 10 Wheel</a> (with <em>Remove Winner After Spin</em> kept OFF):
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Minutes 0–4 (Hypothesis):</strong> Ask students how many times each digit 1–10 should land across 20 spins. Most guess "almost exactly twice each."</li>
          <li><strong>Minutes 4–10 (20-Spin Run):</strong> Set Spin Duration to <code>2s</code>, uncheck the Winner Popup, and let 20 students tap Spacebar once each. Inspect the right-hand <strong>Winner Frequency Stats</strong>—almost every class sees at least one digit appear 4 or 5 times and two digits appear 0 times!</li>
          <li><strong>Minutes 10–15 (Busting the Gambler's Fallacy):</strong> Point to a digit sitting at zero hits and ask: "Is this number now <em>more</em> likely on Spin 21?" Discuss why memoryless Web Cryptography (<code>crypto.getRandomValues()</code>) still gives that digit exact 10% odds.</li>
          <li><strong>Minutes 15–25 (100-Spin Aggregation &amp; Spreadsheet Chart):</strong> Split into five lab pairs running 20 spins each, click <strong>Export CSV</strong> on each Chromebook, combine all 100 rows in Google Sheets, and build a bar chart showing how frequencies flatten noticeably closer to the 10% horizontal line as sample size grows.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
          Want the cryptographic details behind why our rejection-sampling engine avoids subtle modulo skew? Read <a href="/blogs/is-spin-wheel-truly-random">Is a Digital Spin Wheel Truly Random?</a> and compare wheels against physical dice in <a href="/blogs/spin-wheel-vs-other-random-pickers">Spin the Wheel vs. Other Random Pickers</a>.
        </p>

        <h2 id="mistakes">7. Five Mistakes Hosts Make During Live Number Draws</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>1. Leaving 6-second spins active for 75-ball Bingo:</strong> Save slow 6–8s Drumroll spins for the final Grand Prize or Draft Pick #1; drop to <code>2s</code> for rapid multi-round calls.</li>
          <li><strong>2. Pasting blank rows or hidden spaces from spreadsheets:</strong> Quickly glance at the total slice counter above the entries list before spinning (e.g., confirm it says <code>100</code>, not <code>101</code>) so no stray header cell like <code>"Ticket_ID"</code> sneaks onto the wheel.</li>
          <li><strong>3. Refreshing the tab mid-raffle without exporting:</strong> Spin History lives in browser session memory; click <strong>Export CSV</strong> before closing or reloading the tab if you need a permanent audit file.</li>
          <li><strong>4. Forgetting to test projector audio before a crowd arrives:</strong> Our header speaker button toggles audio cleanly between Tick, Drumroll, and Silent—check volume levels with one practice spin (then click <em>Clear History</em>) before going live.</li>
          <li><strong>5. Letting 100+ slices stay unreadable on a tiny phone screen:</strong> When running a 100-number or 350-ticket draw on mobile, flip the phone landscape or cast to a TV in Fullscreen Mode—and always read aloud from the large winning result banner right below the Spin button.</li>
        </ul>

